//? Title: Minimum-cost bounded counterfactuals
//? Lang: en

> Minimum-cost bounded counterfactuals

A linear classifier assigns a score to each input. A counterfactual
asks for the smallest feasible change that reaches a given score.
Here the first two features lie in [0, 1] and the third is immutable.
Moving the first feature costs twice as much per squared unit as
moving the second.

For a fixed nonnegative dual multiplier, each optimal coordinate
is its original value plus the multiplier times its score coefficient
divided by its cost weight, clipped to its bounds. Bisection finds the
smallest multiplier that reaches the target score. A maximizer at the
bounds checks feasibility first. An already acceptable input stays put.

The plot shows the first two coordinates separately for the feasible
rows. The third coordinate remains fixed, so the decision boundary
in this plane depends on the row.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/counterfactual_explanations.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const std::vector<double> weight{1,-1,1},cost{2,1,4};
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> inputs{{.2,.7,.8},{.9,.6,.4},{.2,.4,.1},{.9,.1,.9}};
  constexpr double bias=-1.2,target=.05;
  const auto score=[&](const auto& row) {
    return std::inner_product(row.begin(),row.end(),weight.begin(),bias);
  };
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::print("| Row | Original score | Final score | Cost | Counterfactual |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n");
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<inputs.size();++i) {
    const auto& x=inputs[i];
    const auto solution=dm::nn::linear_counterfactual(x,weight,cost,{0,0,x[2]},{1,1,x[2]},bias,target);
    if (!solution) {
      if (i!=2) return 1;
      std::print("| {} | {:.3f} | Infeasible | — | — |\n",i,score(x));
      continue;
    }
    const auto& y=*solution;
    if (i==2 || score(y)<target-1e-12 || y[2]!=x[2] ||
        (i==3 && y!=x)) return 1;
    double distance=0;
    for (std::size_t j=0;j<y.size();++j) distance+=.5*cost[j]*std::pow(y[j]-x[j],2);
    std::print("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.6f} | ({:.4f}, {:.4f}, {:.4f}) |\n",
        i,score(x),score(y),distance,y[0],y[1],y[2]);
    if (i!=3) plot.lines({x[0],y[0]},{x[1],y[1]},std::format("Row {} path",i),
                        i==0 ? dm::graphics::RGB{37,99,235} : dm::graphics::RGB{234,88,12});
    plot.points({y[0]},{y[1]},std::format("Row {} solution",i),{31,41,55});
  }
  plot.title("Feasible changes in mutable coordinates").x_label("Feature 1").y_label("Feature 2")
      .x_limits(0,1.05).y_limits(0,1);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.size(900,430).save_svg("build/counterfactual_explanations.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Row | Original score | Final score | Cost | Counterfactual |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| 0 | -0.900 | 0.050 | 0.300833 | (0.5167, 0.0667, 0.8000) |
| 1 | -0.500 | 0.050 | 0.111250 | (1.0000, 0.1500, 0.4000) |
| 2 | -1.300 | Infeasible | — | — |
| 3 | 0.500 | 0.500 | 0.000000 | (0.9000, 0.1000, 0.9000) |
\image(figures/counterfactual_explanations.svg)
// result_end

For row 0, the unconstrained multiplier is 0.95/1.5, and both
mutable coordinates stay inside the box. For row 1, the first
coordinate hits its upper bound, leaving the second to reach 0.15.
Row 2 can reach at most −0.1, below the requested 0.05.
